\title{Limits of Coarse Invariants in a Periodic Classification of Finite Groups}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 17, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
A periodic classification of finite groups seeks compact coordinates that organize large families and predict structural behavior. Order and exponent are natural candidates. We establish an infinite obstruction: for every odd integer $n>1$, the cyclic group $C_{2n}$ and the dihedral group $D_{2n}$ have the same order $2n$ and exponent $2n$, but the former is cyclic and commutative whereas the latter is neither. Their centers are maximally different: $Z(C_{2n})=C_{2n}$ and $Z(D_{2n})=\{e\}$. The smallest witness occurs at order six. We also derive $|\Aut(C_m)|=\varphi(m)$, and hence $|\Aut(C_6)|=2$. The results show that coarse numerical coordinates cannot determine elementary structure. We discuss composition factors versus extension data, algorithms for collision testing, and a layered fingerprint for finite-group classification.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

The periodic table of chemical elements is valuable not merely because it lists objects, but because its coordinates organize behavior. An analogous program for finite groups would organize the algebraic structures governing finite symmetry. Group order is an obvious ``atomic number,'' while exponent summarizes the cycle lengths of all elements. Composition factors may be treated as irreducible constituents. The strongest ambition is predictive: groups assigned the same coarse coordinates should share important behavior.

Prediction requires adequate features. If two groups agree on every supplied coordinate but disagree on the target property, no rule based only on those coordinates can always succeed. We exhibit infinitely many such collisions for the pair consisting of order and exponent. The examples are elementary, parametric, and structurally sharp.

For every odd $n>1$, compare the cyclic group of order $2n$ with the full symmetry group of a regular $n$-gon. Both have order $2n$. The exponent of the cyclic group is $2n$, while that of the dihedral group is $\lcm(n,2)=2n$. Nevertheless, one group is abelian and generated by one element; the other is nonabelian and noncyclic. Their centers have orders $2n$ and $1$ respectively.

The conclusion is constructive. A useful periodic organization should employ a layered fingerprint. Composition factors describe simple layers, but extension data describes their assembly. Center order, derived length, nilpotency class, exponent, and automorphism-group order contribute complementary information. In computational classification, the family below is a feature-selection benchmark: a representation that collapses these pairs cannot predict the properties on which they differ.

\section{Definitions}

\begin{definition}
A \emph{finite group} is a group whose underlying set has finitely many elements. Its \emph{order}, denoted $|G|$, is its number of elements. A group is \emph{cyclic} if $G=\langle g\rangle$ for some $g\in G$. Up to isomorphism, the cyclic group of order $m$ is denoted $C_m$.
\end{definition}

Every cyclic group is commutative: if $x=g^a$ and $y=g^b$, then $xy=g^{a+b}=g^{b+a}=yx$.

\begin{definition}
For $n\geq1$, the \emph{dihedral group} $D_{2n}$ is the full symmetry group of a regular $n$-gon. It has presentation
\[
D_{2n}=\langle r,s\mid r^n=e,\ s^2=e,\ srs=r^{-1}\rangle.
\]
It contains the $n$ rotations $r^k$ and the $n$ reflected symmetries $sr^k$, so $|D_{2n}|=2n$.
\end{definition}

Our subscript records the group order; some conventions write $D_n$ for the same group.

\begin{definition}
The \emph{order} $\ord(g)$ of an element is the least positive $k$ with $g^k=e$. The \emph{exponent} of a finite group is
\[
\expn(G)=\lcm\{\ord(g):g\in G\}.
\]
Equivalently, it is the least positive $m$ such that $g^m=e$ for every $g\in G$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}
A group is \emph{commutative} or \emph{abelian} if $xy=yx$ for all $x,y\in G$. Its \emph{center} is
\[
Z(G)=\{z\in G:zg=gz\text{ for every }g\in G\}.
\]
An \emph{automorphism} is an isomorphism from $G$ to itself; all automorphisms form the group $\Aut(G)$.
\end{definition}

Euler's totient $\varphi(m)$ counts residue classes modulo $m$ that are relatively prime to $m$.

\section{Orders and exponents}

\begin{lemma}[Order of the comparison groups]
For every positive integer $n$,
\[
|C_{2n}|=|D_{2n}|=2n.
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The first equality is the definition of $C_{2n}$. Every element of $D_{2n}$ has a unique form $r^k$ or $sr^k$, for $0\leq k<n$. The two classes are disjoint and each contains $n$ elements.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Cyclic exponent]
For every positive integer $m$, $\expn(C_m)=m$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
A generator has order $m$, so the exponent is at least $m$. Every element is a power of that generator and is annihilated by the $m$th power, so the exponent is at most $m$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Dihedral exponent]
For every positive integer $n$,
\[
\expn(D_{2n})=\lcm(n,2).
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The rotation $r$ has order $n$, and every reflection has order $2$, so the exponent is divisible by both $n$ and $2$. Conversely, rotation orders divide $n$. Moreover,
\[
(sr^k)^2=sr^ksr^k=r^{-k}r^k=e,
\]
so every reflected element has order dividing $2$. Thus every element order divides $\lcm(n,2)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
If $n$ is odd, then $\expn(D_{2n})=2n$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Oddness gives $\gcd(n,2)=1$, hence $\lcm(n,2)=2n$.
\end{proof}

\section{The infinite collision theorem}

\begin{lemma}
The group $C_m$ is commutative for every $m$. The group $D_{2n}$ is noncommutative for $n>2$ and noncyclic for $n>1$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Commutativity of a cyclic group follows by adding powers of a generator. In $D_{2n}$, the relation $sr=r^{-1}s$ shows that $s$ and $r$ fail to commute when $r\neq r^{-1}$, which holds for $n>2$. A cyclic group is commutative, so this proves noncyclicity for $n>2$. For $n=2$, all three nonidentity elements have order $2$, so there is no generator of the four-element group.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Coarse-coordinate separation]
Let $n>1$ be odd. Then
\[
|C_{2n}|=|D_{2n}|=2n,
\qquad
\expn(C_{2n})=\expn(D_{2n})=2n.
\]
Nevertheless, $C_{2n}$ is cyclic and commutative, while $D_{2n}$ is neither cyclic nor commutative.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The order statement follows from the counting lemma. The cyclic exponent is $2n$, and the odd dihedral exponent is $\lcm(n,2)=2n$. The cyclic group is cyclic by definition and therefore commutative. Since odd $n>1$ implies $n\geq3$, the preceding lemma makes the dihedral group noncommutative and noncyclic.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Impossibility of prediction]
No function of the pair $(|G|,\expn(G))$ correctly determines cyclicity for every finite group. Likewise, no function of this pair correctly determines commutativity for every finite group.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
For each odd $n>1$, the theorem supplies two groups with identical input $(2n,2n)$ and opposite values of either target property. A function cannot assign two outputs to one input.
\end{proof}

There are infinitely many such collisions, occurring at orders $6,10,14,18,\ldots$. The obstruction is information-theoretic: neither additional data nor a more complicated classifier can reconstruct distinctions erased by the representation.

\section{Maximal separation of centers}

\begin{theorem}[Center of a cyclic group]
For every positive integer $m$, $Z(C_m)=C_m$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Every cyclic group is commutative, so every element commutes with every group element.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Center of an odd dihedral group]
If $n>1$ is odd, then $Z(D_{2n})=\{e\}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Suppose that a rotation $r^k$ is central. It commutes with $s$, but conjugation by $s$ gives $sr^ks=r^{-k}$. Hence $r^k=r^{-k}$, so $n$ divides $2k$. Since $n$ is odd, $n$ divides $k$, and $r^k=e$.

A reflected element $sr^k$ cannot be central. If it commuted with $r$, then
\[
(sr^k)r=sr^{k+1}=r(sr^k)=sr^{k-1},
\]
which would imply $r^2=e$. This contradicts the odd order $n>1$ of $r$. Thus only the identity is central.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Maximal central separation]
For every odd $n>1$,
\[
|Z(C_{2n})|=2n,
\qquad
|Z(D_{2n})|=1.
\]
Thus identical order and exponent are compatible with the two extreme forms of central structure.
\end{corollary}

\section{The order-six laboratory}

Taking $n=3$ compares $C_6$ with the six symmetries of an equilateral triangle. Both groups have order and exponent six. Write
\[
C_6=\{e,g,g^2,g^3,g^4,g^5\},\qquad g^6=e.
\]
Every product is addition of exponents modulo six. For the triangle group,
\[
D_6=\{e,r,r^2,s,sr,sr^2\},
\]
where $r^3=s^2=e$ and $sr=r^{-1}s$. Thus $sr\neq rs$. The first group is cyclic and abelian with six central elements; the second is noncyclic and nonabelian with one central element.

\begin{theorem}[Automorphisms of a cyclic group]
For every positive integer $m$,
\[
|\Aut(C_m)|=\varphi(m).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Choose a generator $g$. An automorphism is determined by the image of $g$, and this image must also generate $C_m$. The element $g^k$ generates exactly when $\gcd(k,m)=1$. Conversely, every such $k$ defines an automorphism by $g^a\mapsto g^{ka}$. There are $\varphi(m)$ choices.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
The cyclic group of order six has exactly two automorphisms:
\[
|\Aut(C_6)|=\varphi(6)=2.
\]
\end{corollary}

The generators are $g$ and $g^5=g^{-1}$, corresponding to the identity and inversion on a chosen generator.

\section{Composition factors and assembly}

\begin{definition}
A \emph{composition series} of a finite group is a chain
\[
\{e\}=G_0\triangleleft G_1\triangleleft\cdots\triangleleft G_k=G
\]
in which each quotient $G_{i+1}/G_i$ is simple. These quotients are the \emph{composition factors}.
\end{definition}

The multiset of composition factors is independent of the chosen series, so it is a natural column label. However, a column defined by equal factors shares those factors by definition; the substantive question is which other properties follow.

Composition factors record layers, not gluing. \emph{Extension data} records how a quotient acts on a normal subgroup and how the layers combine. The order-six pair illustrates the distinction. Both groups possess prime-order layers of sizes $2$ and $3$. In the cyclic group the interaction is trivial and commutative. In the dihedral group the order-two reflection acts on the order-three rotation subgroup by inversion. The same constituent sizes therefore support different multiplication laws.

Consequently, composition factors alone should not be expected to determine commutativity, nilpotency, derived length, center, or automorphism-group order. A periodic classification should expose these independence phenomena.

\section{Algorithms and computational tests}

\subsection{Enumerating the counterexample family}

Given an order bound $B$, enumerate odd $n>1$ with $2n\leq B$. For each $n$, output the common order $2n$, cyclic exponent $2n$, dihedral exponent $\lcm(n,2)$, cyclicity and commutativity flags, and center orders $2n$ and $1$. The loop takes $O(B)$ iterations and $O(1)$ auxiliary space apart from output. Computing a least common multiple through the Euclidean algorithm takes $O(\log n)$ arithmetic steps per row.

\subsection{Counting cyclic automorphisms}

To obtain $|\Aut(C_m)|$, count $k$ in $1\leq k\leq m$ with $\gcd(k,m)=1$. Direct counting takes $O(m\log m)$ elementary arithmetic time. If a factorization is known, one may use
\[
\varphi(m)=m\prod_{p\mid m}\left(1-\frac1p\right).
\]

\subsection{Auditing a proposed fingerprint}

For a database of finite groups, choose a fingerprint $F(G)$ and property $P(G)$. Hash groups into buckets of equal fingerprints. A bucket is \emph{impure} if its members have different values of $P$. Every impure bucket proves that $F$ does not determine $P$ on the dataset. For hashable records, $N$ groups can be processed in expected time and space $O(N)$. The separation theorem supplies analytically certified impure buckets for $F(G)=(|G|,\expn(G))$ and targets cyclicity or commutativity.

\section{A layered periodic schema}

A robust classification may assign the fingerprint
\[
\mathcal F(G)=\bigl(
|G|,\ \text{composition factors},\ \text{extension data},\
\expn(G),\ |Z(G)|,\ \text{derived length},\
\text{nilpotency class},\ |\Aut(G)|
\bigr).
\]
The entries have complementary roles:
\begin{enumerate}[label=\arabic*.]
\item order measures size and restricts possible subgroup indices;
\item composition factors identify irreducible layers;
\item extension data describes interactions between layers;
\item exponent summarizes element orders;
\item center order measures the globally commuting core;
\item derived length measures noncommutative depth in solvable groups;
\item nilpotency class measures central-series complexity;
\item automorphism-group order measures symmetries of the multiplication law.
\end{enumerate}

This is not asserted to be a complete invariant. It is a schema in which collisions become informative. Groups sharing early coordinates but separating later demonstrate the independence of those later features. A visual table could group first by composition factors, then refine by extension type and secondary invariants.

For machine learning, theorem-driven collisions should accompany empirical accuracy. A model cannot infer a target that its representation provably fails to determine. It may instead report a distribution, request richer features, or limit its claim to a restricted family.

\section{Applications}

\paragraph{Symmetry analysis.}
Cyclic symmetry models repeated motion in one direction; dihedral symmetry adds reversal. Equal size and equal global reset period do not detect the noncommutativity introduced by reversal. Molecular, mechanical, and image symmetries therefore require interaction data rather than cycle statistics alone.

\paragraph{Data representation.}
The map $G\mapsto(|G|,\expn(G))$ sends $C_{2n}$ and $D_{2n}$ to one vector for every odd $n>1$. If labels distinguish abelian from nonabelian, the encoded dataset has unavoidable label collisions. This tests whether a classifier represents uncertainty honestly.

\paragraph{Certified census design.}
A finite census can begin with supplied multiplication tables, check the group axioms, and compute centers, element orders, normal series, and fingerprints. Collision reports should be first-class products because they delimit predictive claims.

\section{Discussion and future work}

The examples identify a mechanism, not a low-order accident. For odd $n$, the rotation period $n$ and reflection period $2$ combine into exponent $2n$, exactly matching the cyclic group. Exponent remembers the least common multiple of local periods while forgetting whether the corresponding motions commute. The center quantifies the resulting information loss: all $2n$ cyclic elements are central, while only one dihedral element is.

Several next steps are natural. One should make the composition series of $C_6$ and $D_6$ explicit, showing that both have factors of orders $2$ and $3$ while retaining the established structural differences. Comparing $|\Aut(C_6)|=2$ with the automorphism group of the triangle symmetry group would test automorphism order within a composition-factor column. Further examples should separate derived length and nilpotency while preserving composition factors.

A census through order $100$ would provide an experimental platform. Rather than unstructured brute force, multiplication tables can be certified and then used to compute normal series and invariants. A collision matrix can record which fingerprints determine which properties in the census and which failures extend to infinite families.

Finally, predictive representations should include extension-sensitive information. Composition factors provide a backbone, but actions between layers are indispensable when targets concern commutativity, centrality, or nilpotency.

\section{Conclusion}

For every odd integer $n>1$, the cyclic group $C_{2n}$ and dihedral group $D_{2n}$ share order and exponent $2n$, yet differ in cyclicity and commutativity. Their centers occupy opposite extremes. At order six, this compares a six-step cycle with the symmetries of an equilateral triangle, and the cyclic group has exactly two automorphisms.

Order and exponent are meaningful coordinates, but they do not encode how symmetries interact. Composition factors record simple layers, while extension data and secondary invariants describe assembly and behavior. A successful periodic table of finite groups must therefore be layered, collision-aware, and explicit about the information each coordinate forgets.

